WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.480
Welcome to Grade 5 English, Unit 4: Telling Timeless Tales.

2
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.860
In this unit you will explore myths and legends —

3
00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:12.660
the timeless tales that people have told
for hundreds and even thousands of years.

4
00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:20.520
Myths are ancient stories that explain why things in nature
happen (such as why the sun rises or why the sea is salty).

5
00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:24.940
They often involve gods, spirits, or supernatural beings.

6
00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:30.500
Legends are stories loosely based on
real events or real people from history,

7
00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:34.880
but with unlikely or exaggerated details added over time.

8
00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:38.940
Both myths and legends have been
passed down through oral tradition,

9
00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:42.600
meaning people told them aloud
long before anyone wrote them down.

10
00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:49.190
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

11
00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:54.780
By the end of this unit, you will be able to
explain the difference between a myth and a legend.

12
00:00:55.260 --> 00:01:01.160
you will also identify the key features of
classic tales: oral tradition, timeless settings,

13
00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:05.580
moral or explanatory purpose, and larger-than-life characters.

14
00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:10.960
you will also use defining relative clauses
to add essential information to your sentences.

15
00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:13.660
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

16
00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:21.460
Your vocabulary work begins with these words: hasty,
reluctant, furious, anxious, grateful, irritable.

17
00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:26.780
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

18
00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:30.280
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

19
00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:39.690
The reading sequence includes Telling Timeless Tales:
source text 1 and Telling Timeless Tales: source text 2.

20
00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:41.100
Read actively.

21
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:45.880
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

22
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.020
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

23
00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:54.040
The first language focus is What
Are Defining Relative Clauses?.

24
00:01:55.260 --> 00:02:00.320
A defining relative clause is a group of words
that gives us essential information about a noun.

25
00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:05.340
It tells us exactly which person,
place or thing we are talking about.

26
00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:09.140
Without the clause, the sentence
would be unclear or incomplete.

27
00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:13.340
Look at this example: Notice the
structure in the model examples,

28
00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:17.120
then apply it deliberately in
connected sentences and paragraphs.

29
00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:22.700
In speaking, you will complete Speaking 1: Retell a Legend.

30
00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:26.940
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

31
00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:30.120
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

32
00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.640
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

33
00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:35.120
You are ready to begin.

34
00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:40.640
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

35
00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:43.080
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

36
00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:49.260
Use AI English for hints and feedback, and
bring your questions to the live sessions.
